  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 134 AM EDT Tue Aug 25 2026\n\n             -----------Synoptic Overview-----------\n\nAside from a pair of troughs across New England and the Canadian\nPrairies, H5 heights are above normal over most of N America. A\nlarge ridge axis extends from the Desert Southwest to the Lesser\nAntilles. Near Florida, a weakness in the ridge continues to be\ncarved out by weak ripples of vorticity. Through the remainder of\nthe week, the pattern looks relatively stagnant. A broad trough\ndeveloping from Hudson Bay to the Ohio Valley should reinforce \nthe weak mid-level feature over the NE Gulf through at least \nFriday. Global ensembles then agree that by next Monday (8/31), a \nlarge mid-level ridge will overtake the Southeast U.S.\n\nWithin this setup, the near-surface ridge axis should remain\nnear or just south of Central Florida through at least Friday. By\nthe weekend and into early next week, low-level onshore flow is\nforecast to become established as the high-pressure axis moves\nnorth of the area.\n\nA ribbon of slightly drier air sits over Central Florida tonight,\nwith PWATs < 1.6\". Guidance suggests moisture will increase \nslightly over the next couple of days and remain near to just \nabove late-August norms thereafter.\n\n             -------Sensible Weather & Impacts-------\n\nToday - Tonight...\n\nWith continued light southwesterly flow and a slight increase in\nmoisture, especially over the northern half of the area, we expect\nan uptick in showers and storms this afternoon and evening. Storms\nshould be able to form on the slowly advancing sea breeze along the\ncoast by early-mid afternoon. Later in the afternoon and evening,\nadditional storm development is favored on boundary collisions with\nthe sea breeze and a diffuse Gulf breeze. This later activity looks\nmore likely along the I-4 corridor. Overall, rain chances range\nfrom 30-40% along the Treasure Coast to 50-70% closer to I-4. With\nlingering dry air aloft, we will be watching for a few strong wind\ngusts from 40 to 50 mph out of the strongest storms. Aside from\nfrequent lightning, locally torrential rain rates over urban areas\nmay lead to localized flooding. Quieter conditions return overnight.\n\nAhead of the storms today, expect widespread low to mid 90s with\nheat indices from 100 to 107 deg F.\n\nWednesday - Friday...\n\nSea-breeze-driven showers and storms are likely each day as\nmoisture values return to normal. A few storms will likely develop\nnear the coast in the early afternoon before a collision over\nthe interior sparks the greatest coverage late in the afternoon\nor by early evening. Weak offshore flow in the mid-levels may\ndrive some lingering storms back toward the east coast in the\nevening. Each day, rain and storm chances range from 50-70%. Our\ngeneral summertime thunderstorm hazards are expected, with frequent\nlightning, wind gusts from 40 to 45 mph, and localized flooding\nthe main threats.\n\nSeasonably hot temperatures and humidity levels will continue.\n\nWeekend - Early Next Week...\n\nRather typical late summer weather is expected to persist as August\nwraps up across east central Florida. Light onshore flow tries to\nbecome established this weekend as high pressure becomes situated\njust to our northeast. With no interruption to the moist airmass\noverhead, expect scattered to numerous afternoon and evening storms,\nwith the focus likely becoming more over the interior with slightly\nless coverage near the coast. Hot and muggy each afternoon before\nstorms begin to form.",
